speechtext:
There was no such intention whatever. The hon. gentleman has dilated upon the fact that the province of Ontario has granted several charters for powers in that same district, with which I quite agree. That shows who is exercising power, namely, the province; the province has, without question by this government, been granting water-powers in that neighbourhood, and in that respect the province of Ontario has pursued very much the same policy that it has with regard to other public utilities and portions of the public domain, namely, that it will regulate and dispose of its powers and its assets as it pleases, and in such a way as to inure to the benefit of the people of the province. That is why they are dealing with these powers, why they are dealing with the power to grant these concessions;
5357	MAY 3, 1909	5358
it is because they have the right to deal with them, and because they are dealing with them in accordance with a well conceived policy which is in the public interest. The hon. gentleman has said that the premier of the province has stated that these powers should be as free as air; precisely, as free as air to the neople that own them. But the hon. gentleman proposes to chain these powers up entirely, and not leave them free to the people of the province. What do the people of the province get? If the hon. gentleman gets his Bill and gets a valuable concession, what do they get in return? What do the people of the Dominion get in return for this exploitation of a part of the public domain? They get nothing. Therefore the hon. gentleman is seeking to contravene that very policy.

I am quite aware that there are associated with these gentlemen some very prominent gentlemen from the west, not in the province of Ontario but in the province of Manitoba. I am not undervaluing these gentlemen, but I am pointing out that the chief promoters are gentlemen from the United States who have sharp eyes on the development of power in the province of Ontario. These gentlemen would not be here at all, neither would my hon. friend be promoting this Bill if the province of Ontario had not a clearly defined policy on this question. They were quite aware that they could not obtain all these large powers from the Ontario government, because that government has distinctly stated that none of these powers shall be given away, either to friend or foe, in the province or in any part of Canada or the United States. It regards these rights and privileges as matters of great value which should be retained for the people. Look at the powers asked for by this company:
(d) acquire, develop, improve, maintain and operate water-powers, use, supply and sell water-power for any purpose, by any means of application, generate, by means of waterpower or otherwise, electricity, heat, light and any other form of energy, and transmit the same by any means to, and supply and sell the same for use in any manner at, any place in Canada and the United States;
(b)	construct, maintain and operate all necessary works, dams, wing dams, canals, intakes, tail-races, channels, conduits, tunnels transmission lines, structures, buildings, machinery, plant, appliances, instruments and devices, and erect and maintain poles and towers, and lay and maintain pipes, cables, wires or other conductors, and connect them with similar lines in other provinces and in the United States;
(c)	manufacture, buy, sell and deal in wood, pulp, paper and articles made therefrom, machinery, engines, motors, electrical apparatus and supplies, and engage in a general manufacturing business.

You cannot conceive of anything that these gentlemen do not wish to do. These are all provincial matters, and I do not agree with the Prime Minister when he says that if both this parliament and the provincial legislature have concurrent power to grant legislation of this kind, there is no difference between this side of the House and that side. I may say for myself that there is a difference, for I think that when both legislatures have this power, the provincial legislature should have the exclusive jurisdiction oyer provincial matters.
